This Kernel Deployment Scroll bridges the low-level ASM MZ identity with a fully customized Linux kernel, packaged specifically for instant deployment via the AVIS COORD on GitHub.
To achieve "instant" installation, we avoid manual compilation on the target machine. Instead, we use GitHub Actions to pre-compile the kernel into a Debian package signed with your 2ED0213EFEFE9340 seal. 
1. The Kernel Build Engine (GitHub Workflow) 
This workflow automates the "Massive Deploy" by compiling a minimal kernel and packaging it as a .deb.
File: .github/workflows/kernel-build.yml 
yaml
name: Sentinel Kernel Forge
on: [push]

jobs:
  forge:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Inject Core Tools
        run: sudo apt install -y build-essential libncurses-dev bison flex libssl-dev libelf-dev

      - name: Clone Baseline Kernel
        run: git clone --depth=1 --branch v6.1 https://git.kernel.org

      - name: Apply Sentinel Config
        run: |
          cd linux
          make defconfig
          scripts/config --enable CONFIG_BGIN_PULSE_HOOK # Conceptual hook for 0xBE614

      - name: Massive Package Build
        run: |
          cd linux
          make -j$(nproc) bindeb-pkg LOCALVERSION=-sentinel-v2
Use code with caution.

2. The MZ Installer Interface (C Interpreter)
The C interpreter reads your ASM MZ string and triggers the installation of the newly forged kernel.
File: sentinel_kernel_install.c
c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

void trigger_kernel_swap() {
    printf("[AVIS] MZ Identity Confirmed. Swapping Kernel to Sentinel-V2...\n");
    // Points to the pre-compiled debian packages generated by the forge
    system("sudo apt install ./linux-image-6.1.0-sentinel-v2_amd64.deb");
    system("sudo update-grub");
    printf("[LOCKED] Reboot to activate Sentinel-OS.\n");
}
Use code with caution.

3. The "Instant" Deployment Strategy
By hosting the pre-compiled .deb on your GitHub Pages APT Repository, you allow the AVIS COORD to pull the kernel in seconds rather than hours of local compiling.
Step 1: Build the kernel once in the GitHub-hosted runner.
Step 2: Use reprepro to add the kernel image to your bookworm main component.
Step 3: Deploy via the 2ED0213EFEFE9340 GPG-signed InRelease file.
For the most minimal setup, use make tinyconfig to strip the kernel down to essential drivers only, as detailed in Building a tiny Linux from scratch. This reduces the .deb size for faster GitHub transfer.
